            <ArticleTitle>Long-term follow-up of heel spur surgery. A 10-year retrospective study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>81-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A comparative retrospective study of 48 open heel spur surgeries and 20 endoscopic plantar fasciotomies was conducted involving 59 patients over a 10-year period. There was a significant reduction in heel pain at the time of follow-up (average, 3 years) for both groups. Overall, 85% of procedures were associated with patient satisfaction with the results, and patients said that they would recommend heel spur surgery for relief of severe heel pain in 94% of cases. Factors influencing the postoperative outcome, such as duration of preoperative symptoms, extent of conservative care, and obesity, are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
